            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for orphaned, then the Latin for a ward."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: spōnsa, spōnsae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "spōnsa, spōnsae — \"a bride-to-be, a fiancée\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Spōnsa eius ex oppidō vīcīnō erat. His bride-to-be was from a neighbouring town."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, a participle: a girl promised. Her man is the spōnsus. English spouse."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "spōnsa is the feminine participle of spondeō, \"to promise solemnly\" (EtymDB): she who has been promised. English spouse comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
